</history><permissions><!--Copyright &#x000a9; 2025 The Authors. Published on behalf of the American Heart Association, Inc., by Wiley Blackwell--><copyright-statement content-type="article-copyright">&#x000a9; 2025 The Author(s). Published on behalf of the American Heart Association, Inc., by Wiley.</copyright-statement><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an open access article under the terms of the <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ext-link> License, which permits use and distribution in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited, the use is non&#x02010;commercial and no modifications or adaptations are made.</license-p></license></permissions><self-uri content-type="pdf" xlink:href="file:JAH3-14-e045359.pdf"/><related-article related-article-type="companion" ext-link-type="doi" id="RA1" xlink:href="10.1161/JAHA.124.037874">10.1161/JAHA.124.037874</related-article><kwd-group kwd-group-type="author-generated"><kwd id="jah311470-kwd-0001">Editorials</kwd><kwd id="jah311470-kwd-0002">diet quality</kwd><kwd id="jah311470-kwd-0003">metabolomics</kwd><kwd id="jah311470-kwd-0004">obesity</kwd></kwd-group><kwd-group kwd-group-type="subject-categories"><title>Subject Categories</title><kwd>Diet and Nutrition</kwd><kwd>Obesity</kwd></kwd-group><counts><fig-count count="0"/><table-count count="1"/><page-count count="4"/><word-count count="2200"/></counts><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>source-schema-version-number</meta-name><meta-value>2.0</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>cover-date</meta-name><meta-value>07 October 2025</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>details-of-publishers-convertor</meta-name><meta-value>Converter:WILEY_ML3GV2_TO_JATSPMC version:6.6.6 mode:remove_FC converted:21.11.2025</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta><notes><fn-group id="jah311470-ntgp-0001"><fn id="jah311470-note-0001"><p>The opinions expressed in this article are not necessarily those of the editors or of the American Heart Association.</p></fn><fn id="jah311470-note-0002"><p>This manuscript was sent to Tiffany M. Powell&#x02010;Wiley, MD, MPH, Associate Editor, for editorial decision and final disposition.</p></fn><fn id="jah311470-note-0003"><p>See article by Hullings et&#x000a0;al.</p></fn><fn id="jah311470-note-0004"><p>For Disclosures, see page 2.</p></fn></fn-group></notes></front><body id="jah311470-body-0001"><p>In nutritional medicine, it is often presumed that identical diets yield comparable biochemical benefits regardless of body size. However, emerging metabolomics research challenges this assumption, suggesting that individuals with obesity may exhibit biologically distinct or blunted metabolic responses to high&#x02010;quality diets. In this issue of the <italic toggle="yes">Journal of the American Heart Association (JAHA)</italic>, Hullings et&#x000a0;al. present robust empirical evidence from the CARDIA (Coronary Artery Risk Development in Young Adults) cohort,<xref rid="jah311470-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref> demonstrating that associations between diet quality and plasma metabolite profiles are significantly weaker among individuals with obesity compared with those with normal weight (<italic toggle="yes">Q</italic>
<sup>2</sup>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.21 versus 0.52, respectively), among 2832 young adults studied during 2005 to 2006.<xref rid="jah311470-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>These findings, derived from untargeted metabolomics profiling of &#x0003e;7000 plasma metabolites, signal more than biochemical curiosity; they compel a clinical paradigm shift. Obesity is now recognized not merely as excess adiposity but as a fundamentally altered metabolic state, characterized by metabolic inflexibility. Recognizing this heterogeneity calls for precision nutrition strategies tailored to individuals&#x02019; unique metabolic phenotypes.</p><sec id="jah311470-sec-0002"><title>METABOLIC INFLEXIBILITY: A HALLMARK OF OBESITY</title><p>While body mass index (BMI) provides a convenient clinical classification, it masks significant heterogeneity in metabolic health. Hullings et&#x000a0;al. demonstrated that dietary quality (measured using the A Priori Diet Quality Score) showed attenuated or even inverse associations with key plasma metabolites in participants with obesity. Specifically, obesity significantly modified pathways related to primary bile acid biosynthesis, steroid hormone metabolism, caffeine metabolism, branched&#x02010;chain amino acids, and linoleic acid metabolism.<xref rid="jah311470-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>Such findings align with other metabolomics studies showing that individuals with obesity experience prolonged postprandial elevations in branched&#x02010;chain amino acids and acylcarnitines, indicative of impaired substrate switching and mitochondrial overload.<xref rid="jah311470-bib-0002" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>2</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311470-bib-0003" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>3</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311470-bib-0004" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>4</sup>
</xref> These metabolic disruptions, predominantly affecting skeletal muscle and liver tissues, predict insulin resistance and hepatic steatosis, reflecting underlying mitochondrial dysfunction.<xref rid="jah311470-bib-0003" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>3</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311470-bib-0004" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>4</sup>
</xref> Clinically, metabolically unhealthy obesity, regardless of BMI, carries up to a 2.5&#x02010;fold increased risk of cardiometabolic events compared with metabolically healthy phenotypes.<xref rid="jah311470-bib-0005" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>5</sup>
</xref>
</p></sec><sec id="jah311470-sec-0003"><title>MICROBIOME&#x02013;HOST INTERACTIONS IN METABOLIC RESPONSE</title><p>Why does diet produce divergent metabolic effects in obesity? The gut microbiome is emerging as a critical modulator of host metabolism. Gut microbiota composition influences bile acid pools, systemic inflammation, and short&#x02010;chain fatty acid production, each critical for nutrient processing.<xref rid="jah311470-bib-0006" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>6</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311470-bib-0007" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>7</sup>
</xref> Obesity, for instance, is associated with reduced levels of tauro&#x02010;&#x003b2;&#x02010;muricholic acid, a natural farnesoid X receptor antagonist, potentially shifting bile acid signaling toward pro&#x02010;inflammatory and insulin&#x02010;resistant states.<xref rid="jah311470-bib-0006" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>6</sup>
</xref> Moreover, transplantation of gut microbiota from human donors with obesity or who are metabolically unhealthy into germ&#x02010;free mice induces dysbiosis and glucose intolerance, reinforcing the causal role of microbiome composition in metabolic health.<xref rid="jah311470-bib-0008" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>8</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>This microbial&#x02013;host interplay may explain the differential diet&#x02013;metabolite associations, particularly for metabolites such as linoleic acid, observed in the CARDIA study.<xref rid="jah311470-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref> Individuals with obesity may exhibit altered fatty acid desaturation or lipid partitioning, disconnecting dietary intake from measurable metabolic outcomes.<xref rid="jah311470-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311470-bib-0003" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>3</sup>
</xref> Rather than indicating dietary nonadherence, these responses suggest inherent biological nonresponsiveness tied to microbiome and metabolic dysfunction.</p></sec><sec id="jah311470-sec-0004"><title>NOT NONRESPONDERS: BIOLOGICALLY MISALIGNED</title><p>Despite acknowledgment of metabolic heterogeneity, authoritative dietary guidelines from the American Heart Association (AHA), United States Departments of Agriculture, Department of Health and Humans Services, and American College of Cardiology largely advocate universal nutritional prescriptions. The AHA&#x02019;s 2000 guidelines emphasized uniform reductions in total fat intake (&#x02264;30% of daily energy) without adaptations for insulin resistance or mitochondrial dysfunction.<xref rid="jah311470-bib-0009" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>9</sup>
</xref> Two decades later, the 2021 AHA Dietary Guidance advanced the field by shifting toward food&#x02010;based patterns and broader cardiometabolic health promotion. However, these recommendations still apply at the population level and do not accommodate differential metabolic responses across individuals.<xref rid="jah311470-bib-0010" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>10</sup>
</xref> Similarly, the United States Departments of Agriculture&#x02019;s 2020 to 2025 Dietary Guidelines promote nutrient&#x02010;dense diets universally while limiting saturated fats and added sugars, yet without stratification by metabolic status.<xref rid="jah311470-bib-0011" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>11</sup>
</xref> Recent guidelines, such as the 2013 AHA/American College of Cardiology consensus and 2023 American College of Cardiology/AHA&#x02010;ESC comparative review, anchor obesity management predominantly to BMI thresholds and caloric deficits, minimally accommodating individual metabolic variability.<xref rid="jah311470-bib-0012" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>12</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311470-bib-0013" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>13</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>Hullings et&#x000a0;al.&#x02019;s CARDIA analysis directly challenges this universal dietary framework. Their findings indicate markedly weaker correlations between dietary quality and beneficial metabolite profiles among individuals with obesity, suggesting fundamental disruptions in nutrient signaling and metabolic flexibility.<xref rid="jah311470-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>Large&#x02010;scale studies further reinforce that BMI alone inadequately defines metabolic health. Approximately 30% of adults with obesity have metabolically healthy obesity, whereas 20% of normal&#x02010;weight individuals experience metabolic dysfunction characterized by insulin resistance and inflammation.<xref rid="jah311470-bib-0003" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>3</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311470-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>14</sup>
</xref> Yet clinical practice and insurance policies still predominantly rely on BMI, perpetuating diagnostic mismatches.</p><p>This misalignment carries profound clinical consequences, fostering stigma that acts as a barrier to effective cardiovascular care. When standardized dietary interventions fail, blame shifts to patients, misconstruing metabolic nonresponsiveness as behavioral noncompliance. Revising dietary guidelines to reflect metabolic diversity can enhance clinical outcomes, restore patient dignity, and improve adherence by reducing stigma rooted in misunderstanding biological variability (<xref rid="jah311470-tbl-0001" ref-type="table">Table</xref>).</p><table-wrap position="float" id="jah311470-tbl-0001" content-type="Table"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Disparate Metabolism Pathway by Obesity Status in the CARDIA Metabolomics Study<xref rid="jah311470-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref>
</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><thead><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Metabolic pathway</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Nonoverweight/nonobese BMI (&#x0003c;25 kg/m<sup>2</sup>)</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Obese BMI (&#x02265;30 kg/m<sup>2</sup>)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Primary bile acid biosynthesis</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Strong positive association</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Attenuated metabolic response</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Steroid biosynthesis</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Favorable metabolite shifts</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">No metabolic shifts</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Steroid hormone biosynthesis</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Improved hormone&#x02010;related metabolites</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">No improvement in hormone&#x02010;related metabolites</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Caffeine metabolism</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Clear inverse relationship with poor diet quality</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Borderline association</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Valine, leucine, and isoleucine biosynthesis (BCAA metabolism)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Lower BCAA levels</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Persistently elevated BCAA levels</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Linoleic acid metabolism</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Higher beneficial linoleic acid metabolites</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Reduced beneficial linoleic acid metabolites</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot id="jah311470-ntgp-0002"><fn id="jah311470-note-0005"><p>Six metabolic pathways demonstrated significant differential enrichment (Fisher's Exact Test &#x0003c;0.05) when stratified by BMI status in the CARDIA Metabolomics Study. Participants with nonobese BMI (&#x0003c;25 kg/m<sup>2</sup>) exhibited stronger or favorable enrichment across bile acid, steroid, hormone, caffeine, BCAA, and linoleic acid metabolism. In contrast, individuals with obesity (BMI &#x02265;30 kg/m<sup>2</sup>) showed attenuated or absent enrichment, persistently elevated BCAA levels, and reduced beneficial lipid desaturation, highlighting obesity&#x02010;associated metabolic inflexibility. BCAA indicates branched&#x02010;chain amino acid; BMI, body mass index; and CARDIA, coronary artery risk development in young adults.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></sec><sec id="jah311470-sec-0005"><title>PRECISION NUTRITION AND METABOTYPING: IMPLICATIONS FOR GUIDELINES</title><p>Despite these contemporary guidelines and statements, current recommendations remain generalized.<xref rid="jah311470-bib-0009" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>9</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311470-bib-0010" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>10</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311470-bib-0011" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>11</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311470-bib-0012" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>12</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311470-bib-0013" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>13</sup>
</xref> Addressing this gap between dietary guidelines and metabolic reality necessitates a shift toward biologically informed precision nutrition. Precision nutrition integrates genomic, metabolomic, phenotypic, and gut microbiota data, offering tailored dietary interventions. Landmark trials by Zeevi et&#x000a0;al. and Mendes&#x02010;Soares et&#x000a0;al. demonstrated that machine learning models incorporating individual variables (microbial composition, baseline glucose, and anthropometrics) significantly outperform standardized dietary plans in predicting postprandial glycemic responses.<xref rid="jah311470-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>15</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311470-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>16</sup>
</xref> Such precision approaches improve glycemic and lipid outcomes, illustrating clinical applicability.</p><p>Furthermore, interventions such as time&#x02010;restricted feeding (demonstrated in preclinical models), meal sequencing, and chrononutrition protocols are emerging as promising strategies to address metabolic inflexibility, including impaired fatty acid oxidation and mitochondrial dysfunction.<xref rid="jah311470-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>17</sup>
</xref> These chronobiologically informed dietary strategies significantly enhance metabolic homeostasis, even in metabolically compromised states, underscoring the clinical promise of precision dietary timing.</p><p>Yet, it is critical to acknowledge that precision nutrition and chrononutrition remain emerging fields. Their integration into routine cardiovascular care requires ongoing validation through robust clinical trials and implementation research. As such, while precision nutrition presents a promising pathway, clinicians and researchers should adopt these strategies cautiously, acknowledging current evidence gaps.</p></sec><sec sec-type="conclusions" id="jah311470-sec-0006"><title>CONCLUSION</title><p>The CARDIA study by Hullings et&#x000a0;al.<xref rid="jah311470-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref> highlights a critical, yet often overlooked, reality in nutritional cardiology: identical diets yield different metabolic outcomes depending on obesity status. Such metabolic disparities, driven by altered mitochondrial function, nutrient signaling, and microbiome interactions, demand reconsideration of population&#x02010;wide dietary recommendations.</p><p>Obesity must be reframed, not as a simple behavioral failing, but as a complex, metabolically distinct state requiring tailored interventions. Precision nutrition approaches, such as metabolite&#x02010;guided dietary modifications targeting branched&#x02010;chain amino acids or bile acid pathways, and chrononutrition protocols, offer clinically actionable strategies. Adopting these biologically nuanced frameworks can enhance cardiovascular outcomes, mitigate stigma as a significant barrier to dietary adherence, and align nutritional science with the complexity of human metabolism.</p></sec><sec sec-type="COI-statement" id="jah311470-sec-0008"><title>Disclosures</title><p>None.</p></sec></body><back><ack id="jah311470-sec-0007"><title>Acknowledgments</title><p>Declaration of generative AI and AI&#x02010;assisted technologies in the writing process: During the preparation of this work, the authors used the AI tool <italic toggle="yes">QuillBot</italic> for grammar checks and paraphrasing. The authors subsequently reviewed and edited the content, and take full responsibility for the final version of the manuscript.</p></ack><ref-list content-type="cited-references" id="jah311470-bibl-0001"><title>References</title><ref id="jah311470-bib-0001"><label>1</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal" id="jah311470-cit-0001">
